PLACES=[
N('baker-boyer-building-walla-walla','Baker-Boyer Bank Building','Walla Walla','wa','civic','standing',46.06629,-118.33959,'exact','view','walk','','1911',
  'Baker-Boyer, founded in 1869, calls itself the first bank in what became the State of Washington. Its seven-story office block at 7 West Main Street, designed by the Seattle twins Louis and Michael Beezer, was finished in 1911 and was Walla Walla\'s first skyscraper. The bank took the lower floors and rented offices on the third to seventh.',
  'Engaged Ionic columns run across the first two stories of the front, with a heavy entablature between the banking floors and the office floors above. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'An office building on Main Street, seen from the sidewalk; who occupies it now was not checked. '+DT,
  [W('Baker Boyer Bank'),L('Pacific Coast Architecture Database: Baker-Boyer Bank Building','https://pcad.lib.washington.edu/building/8769/')],
  brick='no'),
N('dacres-hotel-walla-walla','Dacres Hotel','Walla Walla','wa','hotel','standing',46.06567,-118.34196,'site','view','walk','hotels','1873; rebuilt 1899',
  'Frederick Stine, a blacksmith, opened the Stine House at Fourth and Main in August 1873: Walla Walla\'s first three-story brick building, and with 50 rooms the largest hotel in Washington Territory. After it burned, James E. Dacres rebuilt it from the ruins and reopened it in 1899, with the Main Street front redone in a pre-made cast-iron facade from the Mesker Brothers. It stopped working as a hotel in 1959 and was put on the National Register in 1974.',
  'The cast-iron Mesker front on Main Street, which Wikipedia calls a rare local example and mostly unchanged since 1899; the corbelled brick window arches of the 1873 building still show on the east side. Wikipedia says the ground floor is a live music and events venue called The Dacres and the upper floors are unused. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Seen from Main Street. Whether the ground-floor venue still runs was not checked. '+DT,
  [W('Dacres Hotel'),NR('74001984')],
  brick='?'),
N('marcus-whitman-hotel-walla-walla','Marcus Whitman Hotel','Walla Walla','wa','hotel','standing',46.06785,-118.34089,'exact','open','walk','hotels','1928',
  'In 1927 the town\'s businesspeople, led by Baker-Boyer Bank\'s president W. W. Baker, sold $100 shares to raise their part of a first-class hotel, and work began that September. The Seattle architect Sherwood D. Ford designed it; at 13 stories it is the tallest building in the Walla Walla Valley. After years of neglect it was bought and restored from 1999, the year it was put on the National Register, and it still takes guests.',
  'A reinforced concrete tower faced with buff tapestry brick, with cast-stone and terra-cotta ornament, a tiled roof and a cornice set with urn-like crenellations. SAH Archipedia describes a lobby with a terrazzo floor in an urn-and-flower pattern and a paneled "Georgian" room; how much of that survives the restoration was not checked.',
  '',
  'A working hotel at 6 West Rose Street, with restaurants on the ground floor. '+DT,
  [W('Marcus Whitman Hotel'),L('SAH Archipedia: Marcus Whitman Hotel','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/79215')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('memorial-building-whitman-college','Memorial Building, Whitman College','Walla Walla','wa','inst','standing',46.07089,-118.32960,'exact','view','walk','','1899–1900',
  'Whitman College began as a seminary chartered in 1859 in memory of the missionaries Marcus and Narcissa Whitman. The Memorial Building was put up in 1899 and 1900 with a $50,000 gift from the Chicago philanthropist Daniel K. Pearsons, to a design by the local architect George W. Babcock, who waived his fee. It held classrooms, a chapel and the library (until 1934) and is now the college\'s administration building, the oldest on the campus.',
  'A Richardsonian Romanesque building of cream-colored brick on a stone base, trimmed with gray Tenino stone, under red tile roofs, with a five-story clock tower over a round sandstone arch at the entrance. SAH Archipedia says the tower has hand-wound clocks on all four faces and the inside keeps wooden floors, stairs and balustrades. Interior not checked for visitors.',
  '',
  'On the Whitman College campus at 345 Boyer Avenue, seen from Boyer Avenue and the campus walks; rules for visitors were not checked. Parking on the streets round the campus was not checked.',
  [L('SAH Archipedia: Memorial Hall','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/79216'),L('Pacific Coast Architecture Database: Memorial Hall','https://pcad.lib.washington.edu/building/7734/'),W('Whitman College')],
  brick='no'),
N('court-house-square-walla-walla','Walla Walla County Court House Square','Walla Walla','wa','civic','standing',46.06456,-118.34348,'site','view','walk','','1891, 1906 and 1916',
  'Three county buildings stand together at 315 West Main Street: the Hall of Records of 1891, whose architect is not known; the county jail of 1906; and the courthouse of 1916, designed by Osterman and Siebert and accepted by the county on September 27, 1916. It replaced an 1881 brick courthouse by F. P. Allen, which was pulled down in January 1915.',
  'The courthouse is a three-story Classical Revival building of gray Indiana limestone with a big columned portico; inside, a marble staircase rises to the two Superior Court courtrooms on the third floor (courthouses.co). The Hall of Records stands to its north and the red brick former jail to its south. Interior not checked for visitors.',
  '',
  'Walla Walla County\'s working courthouse, seen from Main Street and Fifth Avenue; opening hours were not checked. '+DT,
  [L('Walla Walla 2020: History of Court House Square','https://ww2020.net/315-e-main/'),L('American Courthouses: Walla Walla County','https://courthouses.co/?p=10955')],
  brick='some'),
N('fort-walla-walla-historic-district','Fort Walla Walla Historic District','Walla Walla','wa','fort','standing',46.0525,-118.358611,'site','view','walk','forts','1858–1906',
  'The permanent Fort Walla Walla was built in 1858 beside the town that had grown up round an earlier post. Troops left on September 28, 1910; the fort briefly trained artillery in 1917 and was handed to the Veterans Administration in 1921. Fifteen army buildings of 1858 to 1906 still stand round the old parade ground, now part of the Jonathan M. Wainwright VA Medical Center.',
  'Officers\' quarters round the parade ground, among them four of 1858 that were built of adobe brick (later altered and given glassed-in verandas), the 1859 quartermaster stables and blacksmith shop, an 1893 granary, an ordnance storehouse and two infantry barracks later used as hospital wards (Register form, 1974). The Fort Walla Walla Museum, in Fort Walla Walla Park just to the southwest, has exhibit halls and a pioneer village of 17 buildings.',
  '',
  'The old fort buildings are on the grounds of a working VA medical center: seen from the roads; rules for visitors on the grounds were not checked. The Fort Walla Walla Museum next door says it is open seven days a week, 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., with the pioneer village closed November 1 to March 1 (museum page read November 2026); park at the museum.',
  [W('Fort Walla Walla'),NR('74001985'),L('Fort Walla Walla Museum: hours','https://www.fwwm.org/visit')],
  brick='?',th=[46.04881,-118.36498],thn='Fort Walla Walla Museum parking, Fort Walla Walla Park'),
N('post-office-walla-walla','U.S. Post Office, Walla Walla Main','Walla Walla','wa','civic','standing',46.06832,-118.34000,'exact','open','walk','','1912–1914',
  'After years of lobbying, a lot was bought in 1910 (local citizens gave $8,000 of its $13,000 cost) and the federal government let the contract in January 1912. The cornerstone was laid on July 26, 1912, and the post office moved in on March 2, 1914. A rear addition came in 1965. It was put on the National Register in 1991.',
  'A two-story building on a raised basement: sandstone below and gray brick above, with round arches of alternating brick headers and stretchers, scrolled keystones of Tenino sandstone, sandstone panels in the arches, bracketed eaves and a low hipped roof. The Register form calls the front unaltered apart from the 1965 rear addition. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A working post office at 128 North Second Avenue; go in during counter hours (not checked). '+DT,
  [NR('91000660')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('green-park-school-walla-walla','Green Park School','Walla Walla','wa','civic','standing',46.07442,-118.32240,'exact','view','walk','','1905',
  'Built in 1905 to ease crowding at the Sharpstein and Baker schools, on land in Green\'s Addition bought from May Green, to a design by the German-born Walla Walla architect Henry Osterman. It was put on the National Register in 1990 and is still an elementary school.',
  'A two-and-a-half-story Renaissance Revival school of rose sandstone and pressed brick of a similar hue (Weston brick, says the Register form), with chimneys of patterned pressed brick. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A working public school at 1105 Isaacs Avenue: look from the street, and not during school hours.',
  [NR('90001604')],
  brick='?'),
N('washington-school-walla-walla','Washington School','Walla Walla','wa','civic','standing',46.06776,-118.35174,'exact','view','walk','','1901',
  'A two-story brick schoolhouse built in 1901 and dominated by a tall Romanesque Revival tower on its front. The Register form says the district closed it in the late 1970s (a housing listing says 1982) and sold it to the city in 1981; the city sold it in 1990 to the Walla Walla Housing Authority, which held it empty when it was put on the National Register in 1991. The Housing Authority turned it into 24 one-bedroom apartments for people 55 and older in 1998.',
  'Brick (brown, says the Register form; it shows red-brown in photographs on Wikimedia Commons) with sandstone trim on a stone base, a brick frieze and cornice, and brick chimneys with panels and corbelled caps; the tower rises in brick stages over the arched entrance. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Apartments run by the Walla Walla Housing Authority, at 517 Cayuse Street (the Register form gives 501): look from the street only.',
  [NR('91001737'),L('Affordable Housing Online: Washington School Apartments','https://affordablehousingonline.com/housing-search/Washington/Walla-Walla/Washington-School-Apartments/10064943')],
  brick='yes'),
N('ymca-building-walla-walla','YMCA Building','Walla Walla','wa','civic','standing',46.06804,-118.33556,'exact','view','walk','','1907',
  'Built in 1907 on a site one block south of Main Street, on Mill Creek, to a simplified Beaux Arts design by Henry Osterman (the Park Service\'s record names Henry B. Gessel instead). The entrance was rebuilt and the cornice taken off in 1948, and a one-story addition went on the south side in 1959. It was put on the National Register in 2015, when its form gave the building as vacant.',
  'Flemish bond brick over a rusticated sandstone base, with segmental sandstone lintels; a double brick staircase of 1948 at the front. What the building is used for now was not checked. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'At 28 South Spokane Street; the owner was not checked. Look from the street. '+DT,
  [NR('14001245')],
  brick='yes'),
N('sharpstein-school-walla-walla','Sharpstein School','Walla Walla','wa','civic','standing',46.06449,-118.32567,'exact','view','walk','','1898',
  'A three-story brick school of 1898, still an elementary school. In 2001 the district took off its 1950s additions and added new wings on two sides, leaving the other two sides untouched; the work won Walla Walla 2020\'s architecture grand prize that year. The architects\' page calls it the oldest school in continuous use in the state; that claim was not checked.',
  'The 1898 brick building, with an original masonry arch (once the back door) kept inside the new entry lobby. Interior not checked for visitors.',
  '',
  'A working public school at 410 Howard Street: look from the street, and not during school hours.',
  [L('School Designs: Sharpstein Elementary School, additions and modernization','https://schooldesigns.com/Projects/sharpstein-elementary-school-additions-and-modernizations'),L('Walla Walla 2020: 2001 architecture awards','https://ww2020.net/2001-awards-granted-grand-prize-winner-sharpstein-school-seven-other-major-awards-granted/')],
  brick='?'),
]
